+# `not|never|cannot|n't`, and it REJECTED three true statements of the very limit it protects:
+# "Nothing in phase.js detects a fabricated sha", "No part of the script detects a fabricated sha",
+# "The script is unable to detect a fabricated sha" — a negative subject, a negative determiner, and
+# periphrastic negation, none of which contain any of those four tokens. Two of them are more natural
+# than the sentence build.md actually ships. B1-B3 in negative-test-accept.sh pin this direction; a
+# gate that fails on right writing is the same defect as one that passes on wrong writing.
+# Cost of the broad tokens: a stray "no" or "without" anywhere in the claim's own clause masks it.
+# Accepted, because every regression this sweep has seen was an added clause carrying no negator.
+NEG = re.compile(r"\bnot\b|n't\b|\bnever\b|\bcannot\b|\bno\b|\bnothing\b|\bnone\b|\bneither\b"
+                 r"|\bnor\b|\bunable\b|\bfail(?:s|ed)?\s+to\b|\bwithout\b|\bimpossible\b", re.I)
+# One thing a negator can never be is part of the name of the thing. build.md:218 calls the fabricated
+# sha "a sha it never created", so "A sha the implementer never created is caught by the guard" carried
+# its own alibi and passed (A2h). The OBJ text is blanked before NEG is applied: the words that NAME
+# the object are not a denial that it is acted on. B8 pins that the real negator beside it survives.
