+# is deliberately NOT one — it usually joins verb phrases under one subject and one negation ("does not
+# resolve and detect"), so splitting there would report correct prose. ", and" is, because the comma
+# marks the independent clause.
+# These words are clause boundaries only SOME of the time. " but ", " yet " and " though " also
+# coordinate two adjectives inside one noun phrase, and there the boundary lands between the claim's
+# two halves: "It detects a well-formed but fabricated sha" split into "It detects a well-formed" and
+# "fabricated sha ...", neither of which carries both halves, and acceptance passed on the check's own
+# verb and noun. Which is why the claim is located FIRST and the clause read around it below, rather
+# than splitting the sentence and hoping the claim survives intact. A1d/A1e/A1f pin it; A1g pins that
+# a boundary outside the claim still stops the negator reaching it.
+# A comma followed by a finite verb is a boundary too: it is the non-final member of a verb series,
+# a separate predication under the same subject. "the script resolves the reported value, finds no
+# object of that name, and stops the phase" is three, and reading them as one clause let the middle
+# one's "no" deny the first one's claim (A7). ", and" was already a boundary; this is its siblings.
+# The lookahead is what keeps it narrow — ", which this script cannot do" is not a verb, so
+# phase.js's counterfactual keeps the negator that makes it true.
+CLAUSE = re.compile(r"[;:]|—|--|\s+but\s+|\s+yet\s+|\s+however\b|\s+whereas\b|\s+although\b"
+                    r"|\s+though\s+|\s+while\s+|,\s+(?:and|or|so)\s+|,\s+(?=\w+(?:s|ed)\s)")
+# Every boundary above is identified by what STARTS the next clause. The comma that CLOSES a fronted
+# subordinate clause is identified by what precedes it instead, and what follows it is a subject noun
+# phrase, so the lookahead misses it — which left the commonest sentence shape in English reading as
+# one clause, with the subordinate clause's negator answering for the main clause's claim. Both of
+# these passed acceptance: "Because the manual path cannot do this, the script detects a fabricated sha
+# and stops the phase." (A1c's sentence with the conjunction moved to the front) and "If the
+# implementer reports a sha it did not create, the script detects the fabrication and stops the phase."
+# A1h/A1i pin them. When a statement OPENS with a subordinator, its first comma is a boundary.
+# Adding a boundary can only shrink the span a negator is read over, so this direction cannot mask a
+# claim; what it can do is reject a fronted sentence whose negator is genuinely in the other clause,
+# and a sentence of that shape is the defect, not the writing.
+FRONTED = re.compile(r"[\W\d_]*(?:if|because|when|whenever|while|whilst|although|though|unless|since"
+                     r"|until|after|before|once|whereas|provided|assuming|given|where|wherever"
+                     r"|even\s+if|even\s+though|as\s+long\s+as|so\s+that|now\s+that|in\s+case)\s", re.I)
