+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nThere is no check that detects a fabricated sha.\n"'
+
+accepts "B6 the resolve limit stated with a negative subject" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nNothing resolves a sha, because a Workflow script has no shell.\n"'
+
+accepts "B7 the resolve limit stated of the script itself" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nThe script never resolves the reported sha; it discards it in favour of the real head.\n"'
+
+accepts "B8 the limit with the sha named as build.md names it" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nA sha the implementer never created is not detected.\n"'
+
+# B9-B12 are the direction B1-B8 could not see. Every case above is a sentence ABOUT the sha limit, so
+# all of them agree with a check that has stopped asking whether the sentence is about a sha at all —
+# and the sweep's ACT/OBJ arms had. These are ordinary skill prose on other subjects, in the register
+# this skill already writes (build.md:155 "That fakes the signal"): a reviewer catching invented work,
+# triage rejecting findings, a gate catching a faked green run. None of them says anything about a sha,
+# none of them is false, and each one failed acceptance with "the shipped skill claims the fabrication
+# is detected" — a FAIL that also misdiagnoses the sentence it names.
+accepts "B9 an ACT on a fabricated NON-sha object ('spots a fabricated gate result')" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nA reviewer that spots a fabricated gate result must escalate rather than fix it.\n"'
+
+accepts "B10 the same with 'invented' ('rejects invented findings')" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nTriage rejects invented findings before the count.\n"'
+
+accepts "B11 the same with 'faked' ('catches faked green runs')" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nThe gate catches faked green runs.\n"'
+
+accepts "B12 the same with 'made-up' and 'forged'" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nIdentify made-up citations before you ship.\n\nA reviewer that discovers a forged gate result must escalate.\n"'
+
+# The counterweight to A1h/A1i. A fronted subordinate clause is a boundary, not a verdict: the limit
+# stated in that shape, with the negator where it belongs, must still be accepted. Without this the
+# cheapest way to pass A1h is to reject every fronted sentence that carries the claim.
+accepts "B13 the limit in the fronted shape, negated in the main clause" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nBecause a Workflow script has no shell, no check detects a fabricated sha.\n"'
+
+# The counterpart to A3, and the reason the script-path check cannot simply require every path to
+# resolve inside the skill: two shipped scripts already point at this repository's harnesses on
+# purpose, and that is useful to a reader as long as the sentence says where to look.
+accepts "B4 a host-repo path whose own sentence says it is in this repository" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nThis repository\x27s scripts/negative-test.sh breaks phase.js fifteen ways.\n"'
+
+echo "== mutation test: a script path the installed skill cannot resolve =="
+
+# The bypass. negative-test.sh ships with this REPOSITORY and not with the skill, so a reader of the
+# installed copy at ~/.claude/skills/three-loop-workflow/ has no such file. The host-root fallback in
+# the check accepted it anyway.
+apply "A3 the skill names a script that exists only at the host repo root" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nVerify the harness with `scripts/negative-test.sh` before you close.\n"' \
+  "names a script that does not exist"
+
+# The original defect (edcc26d7): a path that resolves nowhere at all. Pinned so dropping the fallback
+# cannot be undone by loosening the extraction instead.
+apply "A4 the skill names a script that exists nowhere" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nCheck the plan with `scripts/verify-plan.sh` first.\n"' \
+  "names a script that does not exist"
+
+# The bypass, one file class over. The check read `--include='*.md'`, so the same misrouting inside a
+# shipped SCRIPT was invisible — and the skill ships two of those. A reader of phase.js's comments is
+# misled by an unresolvable path exactly as a reader of build.md is.
+apply "A5 a shipped SCRIPT, not a document, names a script that exists nowhere" \
+  three-loop-workflow/scripts/phase.js \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n// See scripts/verify-plan.sh for how to check the plan before you start.\n"' \
+  "names a script that does not exist"
+
+# The exemption must not become a blanket one. Saying "in this repository" tells the reader where to
+# look; it does not make the file exist, and the path still has to resolve at the host root.
+apply "A6 a nonexistent path wearing the host-repo marker" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nSee scripts/verify-plan.sh in this repository for how.\n"' \
+  "names a script that does not exist"
+
+# A6b is A8's cause in the other check: both split statements the same way, so a list collapses here
+# too and the host-repo marker in one bullet exempts a path named in another. That is A3 — a path the
+# installed skill cannot resolve — reached through list formatting. The marker has to be in the
+# sentence that names the path, or it tells the reader nothing about that path.
+apply "A6b the host-repo marker in a different bullet from the path it exempts" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\n- run `scripts/negative-test.sh` before you close\n- this repository ships the harnesses\n"' \
+  "names a script that does not exist"
+
+# A9/A9b are A4 — a path that resolves nowhere — worn in the two shapes the extractor did not read.
+# It matched `scripts/NAME.js` and `scripts/NAME.sh` and nothing else, under a header and an ok line
+# that both say EVERY scripts/ path the shipped skill names. A reader sent to scripts/verify-plan.py
+# or scripts/lib/verify-plan.js looks in the installed skill's scripts/ directory and finds nothing,
+# exactly as A4's reader does, and cannot tell an invented tool from a missing one. This is 77cda1b's
+# defect — a header claiming more than the extraction covers — one axis over.
+apply "A9 the skill names a scripts/ path with an extension the extractor did not read" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nRun `scripts/verify-plan.py` before you start.\n"' \
+  "names a script that does not exist"
+
+apply "A9b the skill names a scripts/ path in a subdirectory" "$BUILD" \
+  's = s.rstrip("\n") + "\n\nRun `scripts/lib/verify-plan.js` before you start.\n"' \
+  "names a script that does not exist"
+
+echo
+if [ "$fail" -eq 0 ]; then
+  echo "negative-test-accept: every mutation was detected, and every true statement was accepted"
+else
+  echo "negative-test-accept: $fail case(s) failed — accept-release.sh is not asserting what it claims"
+fi
+exit "$fail"
